
  One assumption I'll state rather than ask about, since the repo forces it: [1mthere is no task list yet, so a minimal one
[0m  [1mis part of this work[0m — create a task, give it a title and an optional due date, mark it done, persist to
  [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m. It's a prerequisite, not a separate feature, and it stays deliberately thin. Say so if you'd rather drop
  in your own.

  The remaining fork is where a reminder actually shows up, and it's less cosmetic than it looks — it decides whether a
  reminder can fire when you aren't looking at the page.

  - [1mIn-page only[0m — a banner/toast plus a bell with a count, driven by a timer while the page is open. No permission
    prompt, no service worker, works offline, trivially testable. The cost: close the tab and nothing fires; reopen it
    and you get a pile of "this was due two hours ago." For a demo that's often fine and honest.
  - [1mOS notifications[0m — the [38;5;153mNotification[39m API, so reminders surface outside the browser. Needs a permission prompt, and to
    fire with the tab closed it needs a service worker and [38;5;153mshowTrigger[39m/periodic sync, which is unevenly supported. Much
    more machinery, and it's the part most likely to behave differently across browsers.
  - [1mBoth, in-page as the source of truth[0m — the in-page center always has the full state; OS notifications are a
    best-effort mirror when permission is granted. Slightly more work than in-page alone, but it degrades cleanly and
    you can add the OS layer later without redesigning.

  I recommend the third, built in that order: in-page first as a complete working feature, OS notifications as a thin
  optional layer on top. It gets you something real quickly and doesn't make correctness depend on browser permission
  quirks.
